tion in Ecuador, Peru, Chile and Argentina."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In April 2025 the Corporación Wilfrido Vargas issued a communiqué stating that the name is a registered trademark belonging to the firm, and that promotional or artistic activity using it requires prior formal authorisation; the same document acknowledged that the group had originally been formed on Belkis Concepción’s initiative. Vargas then withdrew from concerts Omar Enrique had scheduled in Venezuela for that June, and Enrique, producing signed contracts, announced he would sue in both countries. The corporation repeated its statement in May 2026."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Legacy","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\"El negro no puede\" returned to public attention in May 2010, when Shakira released \"Waka Waka (Esto es África)\" with the same chorus. Both drew on \"Zangalewa\", a Cameroonian marching song popularised by the group Golden Sounds, which is where Vargas had taken it; he said he would not sue, since the song was not his. Las Chicas del Can were the first fully female merengue orchestra in the Dominican Republic, and out of their ranks Miriam Cruz, Verónica Medina and Michell Flores each left to record on her own."}]}]}'::jsonb, 'published', id, 1 FROM artists WHERE slug = 'las-chicas-del-can'
